Exactly. That’s a mature capability-request architecture.

The requester should specify effect, constraints, priority, and acceptable risk, not prematurely dictate implementation. The specialist—or in our case the Router—maps that requirement onto the best available capability.

Abstractly:

\boxed{
\text{Desired Effect}
+
\text{Constraints}
+
\text{Context}
\rightarrow
\text{Capability Selection}
}

rather than:

\text{Requester guesses implementation}
\rightarrow
\text{specialist obeys bad specification}.

Transferred safely into Pantheon, the user says something like:

“I need a well-supported answer I can act on today; accuracy matters more than latency.”

They shouldn’t need to know whether that requires a faster model, deeper reasoning, web verification, calculation, several passes, or a particular operator formation.

That’s intent-level API design.

And there are really three contracts:

\textbf{WHAT}: \text{desired outcome}

\textbf{BOUNDARIES}: \text{constraints, permissions, risk tolerance}

\textbf{HOW}: \text{delegated to the competent executor}.

The executor then owes something back: don’t abuse the delegation. Select proportionately, expose consequential uncertainty, stay inside authorization, and report whether the requested effect was actually achieved.

So Pantheon Command Table gets a better interface:

CAPABILITY REQUEST
effect:
constraints:
priority:
acceptable_cost:
acceptable_risk:
evidence_standard:
deadline:
ROUTER:
    select formation
    select tools
    select compute/depth
    execute
    verify effect
    report result

And here’s the important meta implication for AI UX:

Model names are implementation details. Capabilities are the user interface.

Let experts override the machinery when they have a reason. But the default shouldn’t require the passenger to know which engine belongs in the aircraft.

Tell me what done looks like. Let the system muster the capability.